@brief
    SOFT-12  Soft delete went in last month and the reads were never
             updated. Every `where` clause in every repository is
             returning deleted rows. Support have noticed; two customers
             have seen cancelled orders come back.

Every `where: { ... }` in the six repositories gains `, deleted: null`
before its closing brace:

    where: { customerId },          becomes   where: { customerId, deleted: null },
    where: { email, active: true }, becomes   where: { email, active: true, deleted: null },

Eight clauses, six files, all open. `docs/soft-delete.md` is also open —
it is the migration note, and it quotes a `where` clause as an example of
what the code used to look like. That quote stays exactly as it is.

**The budget is 98 keystrokes.** Editing eight lines by hand is around
four hundred, and the last one is in the file you would have stopped
before reaching.

@keys
| `:argdo cmd` | run cmd in every file in the argument list |
| `:argd name` | drop a file from the argument list |
| `:args` | see what is in the list |
| `:%s/\(pat\)/\1 extra/ge` | keep the match, add to it, and do not complain when there is none |

@hints
Seven files are open and six of them want the same change. That is what
the argument list is for — but the seventh is in the list too, and
`:argdo` will not ask.
--
Take the documentation out of the list before you run anything. `:argd`
removes a file from the argument list without closing it, and `:args`
shows you what is left.
--
The substitution has to keep the contents of the clause and add to it, so
it needs a group and a backreference. The `e` flag stops `:argdo`
aborting on the file that has no match.

@solution
`:argd docs/soft-delete.md`, then
`:argdo %s/\(where: { [^}]*\) }/\1, deleted: null }/ge`, then `:wqa`.
85 keystrokes.

**`:argd` is the whole judgement.** Everything else here is mechanical.
`:argdo` is a blunt instrument by design — it does the same thing to
every file you have told it about — so the safety is in what the list
contains, not in what the command does. One keystroke's worth of thought
about the list beats a clever pattern that tries to recognise a Markdown
code block.

This is the same idea as choosing the glob in a `:vimgrep`, and it is
worth stating plainly: **with a batch command, scope the set first and
keep the operation simple.** The alternative — one set, one complicated
operation full of exceptions — fails in a way nobody can review.

**The pattern keeps what it matched.** `\(where: { [^}]*\)` captures the
clause up to but not including the closing brace, and `\1, deleted: null }`
puts it back with the filter appended. The `[^}]` is doing real work: it
cannot cross a brace, so the match is confined to one clause on one line
and there is no way for it to run off the end of a short object into the
next one. When you write a batch substitution, the character class that
says "and not past here" is the part to get right.

**The `e` flag.** Without it, the first file with no match raises E486 and
`:argdo` stops there, leaving half the tree edited and no error you would
necessarily notice in a terminal full of output. `e` means "no match is
not an error", and any `:argdo`, `:bufdo` or `:cfdo` substitution that
runs over a set you did not hand-pick should have it.

Afterwards: `:vimgrep /where: {/gj src/**/*.ts` and read the list. Eight
clauses, eight filters. On a change that touches every read in the
application, the thirty seconds spent confirming that is the cheapest
thirty seconds of the ticket.

@start src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public async findOneByReference(reference: string) {
    const record = await this.prisma.order.findFirst({
      where: { reference },
    });

    return record ? this.toEntity(record) : null;
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public async findOneByReference(reference: string) {
    const record = await this.prisma.order.findFirst({
      where: { reference, deleted: null },
    });

    return record ? this.toEntity(record) : null;
  }
}

